## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of practical ES+CQRS integration across microservices with a lightweight saga.

## Key Concepts
- Event Sourcing: immutable event log
- CQRS: separate read model for queries
- Saga/Orchestrator: cross-service workflow with compensations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal orchestrator sketch
function handleEvent(event) {
  switch (event.type) {
    case 'PostCreated':
      publish('ModerationRequested', { postId: event.data.postId });
      break;
    case 'ModerationResult':
      if (event.data.result === 'Approved') {
        publish('PostPublished', { postId: event.data.postId });
        publish('NotifyFollowers', { postId: event.data.postId });
      } else {
        publish('PostRejected', { postId: event.data.postId, reason: event.data.reason });
      }
      break;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement idempotent event processing in the Moderation service?
- What are the trade-offs of using a separate read model vs. a consolidated repository for listings of visible posts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical OAuth2 debugging skills, understanding of token lifecycle, and real-world troubleshooting abilities that backend engineers face daily.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates identify: 1) Refresh token expired/revoked, 2) Scope mismatch between access and refresh tokens, 3) Client configuration issues. They should mention checking token introspection endpoint, reviewing logs, and testing with Postman/curl.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Debug refresh token failure
async function debugRefreshToken(refreshToken: string) {
  try {
    // 1. Check token introspection
    const introspect = await fetch('/oauth/introspect', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: `token=${refreshToken}`
    });

    // 2. Validate client configuration
    const clientConfig = await getClientConfig();

    // 3. Review token storage
    const storedToken = await getStoredToken(refreshToken);

    return { introspect, clientConfig, storedToken };
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Debug failed:', error);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of CQRS pattern in real-time collaborative apps - crucial for Miro's architecture where immediate UI feedback and data consistency are both required.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidate will explain: Command side receives shape update request, validates permissions, persists to database, emits ShapeUpdated event. Query side maintains read model for fast UI rendering. Event-driven architecture ensures eventual consistency across all connected clients.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Command
class UpdateShapePositionCommand {
  constructor(public shapeId: string, public x: number, public y: number) {}
}

// Command Handler
class ShapeCommandHandler {
  async handle(command: UpdateShapePositionCommand) {
    const shape = await this.repo.findById(command.shapeId);
    shape.updatePosition(command.x, command.y);
    await this.repo.save(shape);

    // Emit event for query side
    this.eventBus.emit(new ShapeUpdated(shape.id, shape.x, shape.y));
  }
}

// Query side optimized for UI
class ShapeReadModel {
  async getShape(shapeId: string) {
    return this.readDb.shapes.find(shapeId);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle concurrent edits from multiple users?
- What happens if the command fails but the event was already emitted?
- How would you scale the read side for millions of users?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced distributed systems concepts: sagas, event sourcing, CQRS, and failure handling at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration vs choreography trade-offs
- Event sourcing with immutable event streams
- Idempotency via correlation IDs and deduplication
- Compensating actions for distributed transactions
- CQRS separation for read/write optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
// Saga orchestrator pseudo-code
class OrderSaga {
  async handleOrderCreated(event) {
    const { orderId, items, total } = event.data;

    const inventoryResult = await this.reserveInventory(orderId, items);
    if (!inventoryResult.success) {
      await this.compensateWithOrderCancelled(orderId);
      return;
    }

    const paymentResult = await this.authorizePayment(orderId, total);
    if (!paymentResult.success) {
      await this.releaseInventory(orderId, items);
      await this.compensateWithOrderCancelled(orderId);
      return;
    }

    await this.createShipping(orderId, event.data.shippingAddress);
    await this.completeOrder(orderId);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test end-to-end saga correctness across services?
- What tracing and metrics would you collect for correlation across microservices?
- How would you handle saga timeout scenarios and cleanup?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a policy-driven, multi-source token issuance flow with per-tenant constraints and real-time evaluation.

## Key Concepts

- Lightweight policy decision point (PDP) for token issuance
- Per-tenant audience/issuer metadata and key rotation
- Context-based access controls: IP allowlist, geolocation, time window, device fingerprint
- Caching decisions to reduce latency and load

## Code Example

```javascript
// issuance after policy approval (sketch)
function issueJWTForPolicyApproved(payload, tenant, kid, ttlSec) {
  const now = Math.floor(Date.now()/1000);
  const exp = now + ttlSec;
  const tokenPayload = {
    iss: `https://idp.example/${tenant}`,
    aud: `https://api.${tenant}.service`,
    sub: payload.sub,
    iat: now,
    exp,
    nonce: payload.nonce,
    tenant
  };
  return signJWT(tokenPayload, kid);
}
```

