## Why This Is Asked

Contract-driven, multi-protocol API surfaces with per-tenant policies are a common real-world challenge. This question probes how to orchestrate versioning, enforcement, and migration across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC while preserving contract compatibility.

## Key Concepts

- Central, versioned Contract Registry per tenant
- Runtime field visibility enforced in REST, GraphQL, and gRPC adapters
- Canary migrations and cross-protocol compatibility checks
- Contract drift detection and end-to-end validation

## Code Example

```javascript
type Policy = { fieldsVisible: string[] };
function applyPolicy(record, policy: Policy) {
  const out = {};
  policy.fieldsVisible.forEach((f) => {
    if (f in record) out[f] = record[f];
  });
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test cross-protocol compatibility during policy changes?
- What metrics and dashboards would you use to detect contract drift and rollout health?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical cache invalidation strategies in multi-region Redis deployments, testing understanding of versioned cache keys, cross-region synchronization, and TTL management. It also assesses knowledge of Redis pub/sub versus keyspace notifications and write-through caching trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Versioned cache keys with TTL management
- Cross-region invalidation patterns
- Redis pub/sub vs keyspace notifications
- Atomic operations and consistency guarantees
- Write-through vs write-behind caching strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: bumpVersion(id)
const newVer = await redis.incr(`product:${id}:ver`);
await redis.publish('cache:invalidate', JSON.stringify({id, ver: newVer}));
```

```javascript
// Pseudo: getCache(id)
const currentVer = await redis.get(`product:${id}:ver`);
const cached = await redis.get(`product:${id}:v${currentVer}`);
if (!cached) {
  // Cache miss - refresh from source
  const data = await fetchFromDatabase(id);
  await redis.setex(`product:${id}:v${currentVer}`, 120, JSON.stringify(data));
  return data;
}
return JSON.parse(cached);
```

